WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.004
Cabinet Confidence.

00:00:01.954 --> 00:00:03.920
The document blockade.

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:10.511
When parliamentary committees order the government to produce documents, the government can claim cabinet confidence to withhold them.

00:00:10.511 --> 00:00:17.125
This claim is virtually unchallengeable — no court in Canada has ever compelled the production of cabinet confidences.

00:00:17.125 --> 00:00:23.897
The mechanism that is supposed to protect the candour of cabinet deliberations becomes a tool for blocking parliamentary accountability.

00:00:23.897 --> 00:00:27.329
The government decides what Parliament is allowed to see.

00:00:27.329 --> 00:00:29.784
How cabinet confidence works.

00:00:29.784 --> 00:00:35.624
Section 69 of the Access to Information Act excludes cabinet confidences from disclosure.

00:00:35.624 --> 00:00:40.284
The Clerk of the Privy Council certifies which documents are cabinet confidences.

00:00:40.284 --> 00:00:44.079
This certification is not subject to independent review.

00:00:44.079 --> 00:00:48.579
The Information Commissioner cannot examine the documents to verify the claim.

00:00:48.579 --> 00:00:57.693
Parliamentary committees can order document production, but the government can refuse on cabinet confidence grounds — and has done so repeatedly on politically sensitive files.

00:00:57.693 --> 00:01:00.204
The democratic process blockade.

00:01:00.204 --> 00:01:05.443
Orders in Council bypass Parliament on the way in — implementing policy without a vote.

00:01:05.443 --> 00:01:11.147
Cabinet confidence blocks Parliament on the way out — preventing oversight of decisions already made.

00:01:11.147 --> 00:01:14.295
Prorogation shuts down Parliament entirely.

00:01:14.295 --> 00:01:23.874
Together, these three mechanisms allow the executive to act without democratic input, hide its deliberations from scrutiny, and terminate investigations when they get too close.

00:01:23.874 --> 00:01:27.659
This is the structural architecture of the democratic deficit.
